{"title":"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 21","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Heron)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Heron)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Slight_difference_to_Solution_1)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Slight difference to Solution 1)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Sine/Cosine_Law_+_Quadratic_Residues)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Sine/Cosine Law + Quadratic Residues)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Simple_Tabling)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Simple Tabling)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(Easiest_Accessible_to_Everyone_but_Tedious_with_only_pythagorean_therom_and_little_trigonometry_involved_)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (Easiest Accessible to Everyone but Tedious with only pythagorean therom and little trigonometry involved )</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_7_(&lt;30_sec)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7 (&lt;30 sec)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_by_OmegaLearn.org\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 by OmegaLearn.org</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3_by_MadMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 3 by MadMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Two non-congruent triangles have the same area. Each triangle has sides of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and the third side of each triangle has integer length. What is the sum of the lengths of the third sides?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/9/0590323b12658a681ab9e5b9b11b7eddbdca7b7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 20 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 22 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } 24 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 26 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } 28$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"401\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/f/64f91ca19d83a476e55dabf03b0e1155ee2c3742.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(250);  pair A = (0,0); pair C1 = (-9,0); pair C2 = (9,0); pair B = (-4.0/3.0, 4*sqrt(35)/3);  pen pen1 = blue+1.2; pen pen2 = red+1.2;  draw(B--A--C1--cycle, pen1); draw(B--A--C2--cycle, pen2);  dot(&quot;$A$&quot;, A, S); dot(&quot;$C_1$&quot;, C1, SW); dot(&quot;$C_2$&quot;, C2, SE); dot(&quot;$B$&quot;, B, N);  label(&quot;$8$&quot;, (A+B)/2, W); label(&quot;$9$&quot;, (A+C1)/2, S); label(&quot;$9$&quot;, (A+C2)/2, S); [/asy]\" width=\"418\" height=\"205\" />\n</p><p>~Diagram by imosilver\n</p><p>Let the angles between the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> sides be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/d/3dd2b053518a1dc68824d714682ff9df76d8f151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/1/981e552986db2a6a782e1699452472a78544d757.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"15\" />. Relating the two triangles' areas, we get\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/6/08664f32671ba5dda103ba4f60e4bd3efe28975a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\sin{\\theta_1} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\sin{\\theta_2}\\]\" width=\"244\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/b/fcbd35c95be9d567897e7c2a6ca336240dbf786f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sin{\\theta_1} = \\sin{\\theta_2}\\]\" width=\"105\" height=\"15\" />\nFor this to be true, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/7/4176aa4513d22a8a7f481e9cd93aabce6991147b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta_1 + \\theta_2 = 180.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"113\" height=\"17\" /> \nWe will now apply Law of Cosines to get an expression for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/e/7ae90d71491f591ab5a16d19ee7629e8226ec9ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/6/bb6c5ddfd368673c178f3323ce2994cdbc8fbdab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> in terms of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/d/3dd2b053518a1dc68824d714682ff9df76d8f151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/1/981e552986db2a6a782e1699452472a78544d757.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"15\" />. \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/5/a55784f0c32081ce19a326f6945fb3c4acd82565.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[s_1^2 = 8^2 + 9^2 - (2)(8)(9)\\cos(\\theta_1)\\]\" width=\"243\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/e/1ee781dbf652cfbf7d00a8a33203320177f4419c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[s_2^2 = 8^2 + 9^2 - (2)(8)(9)\\cos(180-\\theta_1).\\]\" width=\"297\" height=\"20\" />\nNoting that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/3/b73c6d5c055b04a841618b927f4733a0e7df6458.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos{\\theta_1} = -\\cos{(180 - \\theta_1)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"194\" height=\"20\" />, we can add the two equations to get that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/6/b562d5954c01ca982243e0941edce9f1d908b479.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[s_1^2 + s_2^2 = 290.\\]\" width=\"108\" height=\"20\" />\nWe now need two perfect squares that add to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/c/b2cf40f8ceb7e0ef7fbe35f590c889dd513334b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$290$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" />. After some analysis, we find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/7/e979bd1165d8fad665b4c5f8ac5d7eac979e45c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$121 + 169 = 290$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"130\" height=\"15\" />. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/b/c7bfc971f35091445578329baf9c4738a1d9a966.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_1 = 11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/f/6afd6e01e18e6097b00167681b930574a84238b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s_2 = 13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"17\" />, so our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/0/2806c37596d8137682dd95fe9944c11ce50f6c83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11 + 13 = \\boxed{24}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"119\" height=\"25\" />\nNote that the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/2/ee2e2fa7e072077c3cb7bdfc68abaa2abf1e3229.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos{\\theta_1} = 1/6.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"102\" height=\"20\" />  Also, note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/b/cdb1a08505acd56ff0f26cbb695e825834d3f756.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$289 + 1 = 290$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"112\" height=\"15\" />, but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/6/b7616a230c77f0d9a577e5bca01aa06f1c35c457.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$17$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are not valid side lengths due to the Triangle Inequality.\n</p><p>~lprado\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Heron.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Heron)\">Solution 2 (Heron)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Heron)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the third sides be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />. Say the third sides is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is a dummy variable. Then, by Heron’s Formula, the area of the triangle is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/b/1dbaa838cc67097c910c919ccc062d6d17132f0a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{\\frac{x+17}{2}\\cdot\\frac{x+1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{x-1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{17-x}{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{(17^2-x^2)(x^2-1^2)}\\]\" width=\"462\" height=\"43\" />\nSince the areas are the same, this value when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/4/ed47ef35959c171399e8f18eed4b517223823c04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=a$\" width=\"44\" height=\"8\" /> is the same as this value when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/1/741720281e465fc7461c32d504f6d54c5bcaff7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=b$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" />. In other words,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/b/f2b66288a50ee992e47ba5728c678d3b99b0bb5c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{(17^2-a^2)(a^2-1^2)}=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{(17^2-b^2)(b^2-1^2)}\\]\" width=\"378\" height=\"37\" />\nequivalent to\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/2/2d2853d3fadfe84b3d61bb985db8c02c75d8a424.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(289-a^2)(a^2-1)=(289-b^2)(b^2-1)=c\\]\" width=\"337\" height=\"20\" />\nfor some constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>But if we consider the quadratic\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/1/c01b6f93e67e1fdd6cabe38c664201bedaaf1539.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(289-y)(y-1)=c\\]\" width=\"158\" height=\"18\" />\nwe know that the two roots must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/5/1a585f9a273a7f76da4a4a84a30e90ac023479da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=a^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/0/25010df2f859650ecc80c1a45cd4966248604641.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=b^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"20\" />. Expanding the quadratic, we get\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/c/dfc67c78681ef196f81dadf279dd109efba47730.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y^2-290y+289+c=0\\]\" width=\"188\" height=\"19\" />\nBy Vieta’s Formulas, we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/7/527d7b79109e6147203fcef29e9c4fdacf12bb88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2+b^2=290$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" />. Casework yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/3/4038ff14a554813a4959e8bfd7982ec73ff3d30f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=11,b=13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"112\" height=\"18\" /> (or vice versa) as the only solutions, so the sum is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/2/4d2ddc13cd556ec347cb695c2a846646b5bc7231.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } 24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~ <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:Eevee9406\">eevee9406</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Slight_difference_to_Solution_1.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Slight_difference_to_Solution_1)\">Solution 3 (Slight difference to Solution 1)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Slight difference to Solution 1)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Denote the angle that is between side lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> as angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, the other triangle will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/0/110b42b58ced87c31c1122c6bfe89fd7c48a6685.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180^\\circ - A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"73\" height=\"16\" />, because by the area formula of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/b/d3b665cc9b7bf2ad7f7c9c48c1c156d7f29f11cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}a\\cdot b\\cdot\\sin C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"98\" height=\"38\" />, the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/d/21de315e70fd55ab23ee73e30b0cc8080185bd0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin C$\" width=\"39\" height=\"12\" /> needs to remain constant while <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> stay the same to keep the area the same. Denote the shorter third side as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and longer third side as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>By applying the Law of Cosines, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/6/a96e67c791bccb157b9ade1d7e42fec2f57789e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y^2 = 8^2 + 9^2 - 2 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cos(180- A)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"289\" height=\"21\" />, which gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/2/e02b7e95a99e3c2630b04b09ac522c056fa5df08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y^2 = 145 + 144\\cos A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"164\" height=\"20\" />. Similarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/8/9180101014d6bb2251c98fd4407c63ab15e597e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 = 145 - 144\\cos A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"165\" height=\"18\" />. Since the question says the third lengths are both integers, try testing values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/c/3ac4cdec57e624ace87d67e97a5ed947291f227e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos A$\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" />. Notice when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/7/b874f1066c0de34d895868eaa4be31412eee9a67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos A = \\frac{1}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"80\" height=\"38\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/8/f5824f505a3c0f0abc523cf504fa12fa1f20dd40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y^2 = 169$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"70\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/7/ca72161e9a350a1d4a144c8327c1885fb4064184.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 = 121$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"71\" height=\"17\" />, giving the lengths as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/0/030e19e4e2f1c074127cb50581e8496470c5419f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c6878713578626763c38433b3f4c8c2205ad0c15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> respectively. The required answer is then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/4/9e48124eb9645b70835012d3a86326daf75b5a51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$13 + 11 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } 24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"149\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~Mitsuihisashi14\n</p><p>~ <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/latex.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\LaTeX$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" /> by <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:Eevee9406\">eevee9406</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Sine.2FCosine_Law_.2B_Quadratic_Residues.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Sine/Cosine_Law_+_Quadratic_Residues)\">Solution 4 (Sine/Cosine Law + Quadratic Residues)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Sine/Cosine Law + Quadratic Residues)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>As in other solutions, use sine law to check <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/f/fbf32e01fe36b17b2e170ff96b2390c2e85fcfb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\beta=180-\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"99\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/a/0eaa3d65119d54ae2e386893d1aa791bc7256a8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos\\alpha=-\\cos\\beta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"121\" height=\"18\" />. Apply cosine law <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/b/88b788a23a535df76612d6468b1348ab597e51b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2,y^2=8^2+9^2\\mp 2(8)(9)\\cos\\alpha=145\\mp 144\\cos\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"387\" height=\"21\" /> and and verify integer solutions to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/e/83e5449c63d938ba02c61d2ea4097e6d2a199fb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+y^2=2(8^2+9^2)=290$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"213\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/7/467910c14895b2f594062db33a44906d9ef8bda8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" />, we need <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/b/40b3eed3f5c46141fbce2c48461b1d477f19c263.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos\\alpha&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/e/f8e4ee46e07831d29d8e2b15575edeab7d268c62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,289)=(1^2,17^2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"149\" height=\"21\" /> is not valid. WLOG <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/e/3cef70da76b96b41acf5df461cb2d4ee72c6a988.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\le x&lt;y\\le 16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"122\" height=\"17\" />. To help with the bash, note the quadratic residues mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/c/a9c68bec0cb7169b21d20277b13cb52aaaf4487e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0,1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"28\" height=\"17\" />, so it must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/1/0312e6723f6cdee32a9730284e6197825ca34f97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+1\\equiv 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" />, so we only need to check odd cases not divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6a0b59e0faf752bf2615545d5bf8f65db4741eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=5,7,11,13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"115\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/3/c636e83252d027368438c2cc3e45f537c599a212.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$290-25=265$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"121\" height=\"15\" /> bad\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/7/2778d47b9a2e0445a99f16a763a53b6ff121a5b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$290-49=241$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"121\" height=\"15\" /> bad\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/d/bdd1350975a72383f14874fcf3d3349e708fa54f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$290-121=169$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"130\" height=\"15\" /> good, stop by symmetry.\n</p><p>So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2b1bfe644dc29e68e7e41ad6a9e2f9993e12a07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)=(11,13)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"127\" height=\"20\" />, giving <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/4/184cb2fd2b7c922b8a6a19e91e22818ea543aaf1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y=11+13=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } 24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"216\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~imosilver\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Simple_Tabling.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Simple_Tabling)\">Solution 5 (Simple Tabling)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Simple Tabling)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider arbitrary such triangle with third side \\(x\\). By Heron's formula, the area of this triangle is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/1/101b72d0a51e22f0d32053eb38703ef4efeb2926.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{17+x}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{17-x}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1+x}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{-1+x}{2}\\right)}\\]\" width=\"345\" height=\"53\" />\nBy the triangle inequality, we have \\(2 \\leq x \\leq 16\\), which means there are few enough possibilities of \\(x\\) that tabling becomes a viable strategy. Consider setting up a table such as:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/e/85ed3ac9545b911b423aff641b7da028989b365a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c}     \\text{Row}&amp;17-x&amp;x+17&amp;x+1&amp;x-1 \\\\     \\hline      A_1&amp;15&amp; 19&amp; 3&amp;  1\\\\     A_2&amp;14&amp; 20&amp; 4&amp;  2\\\\     A_3&amp;13&amp; 21&amp; 5&amp;  3\\\\     A_4&amp;12&amp; 22&amp; 6&amp;  4\\\\     A_5&amp;11&amp; 23&amp; 7&amp;  5\\\\     A_6&amp;10&amp; 24&amp; 8&amp;  6\\\\     A_7&amp;9&amp;  25&amp; 9&amp;  7\\\\     A_8&amp;8&amp;  26&amp; 10&amp; 8\\\\     A_9&amp;7&amp;  27&amp; 11&amp; 9\\\\     A_{10}&amp;6&amp;  28&amp; 12&amp; 10\\\\     A_{11}&amp;5&amp;  29&amp; 13&amp; 11\\\\     \\hline     B_{1}&amp;4&amp;  30&amp; 14&amp; 12\\\\     B_{2}&amp;3&amp;  31&amp; 15&amp; 13\\\\     B_{3}&amp;2&amp;  32&amp; 16&amp; 14\\\\     B_{4}&amp;1&amp;  33&amp; 17&amp; 15\\\\     \\end{array}\\]\" width=\"300\" height=\"320\" />\nAs \\(x\\) goes from \\(2\\to 16\\), we know the area will increase, peak, and then decrease. Thus, the two possible side lengths with the same area must occur on opposite sides of the side length \\(s\\) that maximizes the area. The area is maximized when 8 and 9 are legs of a right triangle (Why?*), giving a side length of \\(\\sqrt{8^2+9^2}=\\sqrt{145}\\). Thus, \\(12 &lt; s &lt; 13\\), and we can divide the table in two parts, split at \\(x=s\\). Denote the top part \\(A\\) and the bottom part \\(B\\).\n</p><p>The final step is to find some rows \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_j\\) of the table that match in product. This is easier than it seems at first if we proceed by eliminating rows from \\(B\\):\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/3/aa334ecc3797e7e1604893ea3ba52bb35b9d1834.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\quad\\bullet$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"10\" /> \\(B_2\\) contains a factor of 31, which is not present in \\(A\\)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/3/aa334ecc3797e7e1604893ea3ba52bb35b9d1834.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\quad\\bullet$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"10\" /> \\(B_3\\) contains 11 factors of 2, which does not occur for any row in \\(A\\)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/3/aa334ecc3797e7e1604893ea3ba52bb35b9d1834.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\quad\\bullet$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"10\" /> \\(B_4\\) contains a factor of 17, which is not present in \\(A\\)\n</p><p>Thus, \\(B_1\\) is the only valid row remaining, occurring at \\(x=13\\). We quickly** find the matching row in \\(A\\) to be \\(A_{10}\\), occurring at \\(x=11\\). Thus, we arrive at an answer of \\(13+11=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 24}\\).\n</p><p>(*) Think about how you would maximize an area using \\(\\frac 12 ab\\sin(C)\\).\n</p><p>(**) As \\(B_1\\) is so close to the maximum, it is likely that the corresponding row in \\(A\\) will be as well, so if we start at \\(A_{11}\\) and work our way up, we find \\(A_{10}\\) on our second try.\n</p><p>~Jackson La Vallee\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28Easiest_Accessible_to_Everyone_but_Tedious_with_only_pythagorean_therom_and_little_trigonometry_involved_.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(Easiest_Accessible_to_Everyone_but_Tedious_with_only_pythagorean_therom_and_little_trigonometry_involved_)\">Solution 6 (Easiest Accessible to Everyone but Tedious with only pythagorean therom and little trigonometry involved )</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (Easiest Accessible to Everyone but Tedious with only pythagorean therom and little trigonometry involved )\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let's set up a diagram with the side of length 9 being the base. The side of length 8 is placed at an angle \\(\\alpha\\) (\\(0 &lt; \\alpha &lt; 90^\\circ\\)) to the base. If we drop a perpendicular from the top vertex to the base of 9, we form a right triangle where the height is \\(8\\sin\\alpha\\) and the remaining piece of the base for the third side is \\(9 - 8\\cos\\alpha\\). \n</p><p>By the Pythagorean theorem, the third side of this first triangle is the hypotenuse, which evaluates to:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/4/06443584dcf861420a3bbcc0f7ebc889c5cf1d3f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{(9-8\\cos\\alpha)^2 + (8\\sin\\alpha)^2} = \\sqrt{81 - 144\\cos\\alpha + 64(\\cos^2\\alpha + \\sin^2\\alpha)} = \\sqrt{145-144\\cos\\alpha}\\]\" width=\"691\" height=\"33\" />\nTo construct another non-congruent triangle with the exact same area, we can reflect the side of length 8 across that perpendicular height (Why?*). Using a similar method with the Pythagorean theorem, the base becomes \\(9+8\\cos\\alpha\\), and the third side of this second triangle is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/8/0c8ce3d243b9245a5529ca7f994786cbff34e816.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{(9+8\\cos\\alpha)^2 + (8\\sin\\alpha)^2} = \\sqrt{145+144\\cos\\alpha}\\]\" width=\"371\" height=\"32\" />\nBecause the third side is strictly an integer length, \\(145-144\\cos\\alpha\\) must be one of the 12 perfect squares from 1 to 144 (since \\(\\alpha &gt; 0\\) and \\(\\alpha &lt; 90^\\circ\\)). We can use brute-force trial and error to find a value of \\(144\\cos\\alpha\\) that makes both expressions perfect squares. Testing our options, two possibilities for \\(144\\cos\\alpha\\) emerge that satisfy both:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/3/aa334ecc3797e7e1604893ea3ba52bb35b9d1834.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\quad\\bullet$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"10\" /> \\(144\\cos\\alpha = 144\\) yields third side lengths of \\(\\sqrt{145-144}=1\\) and \\(\\sqrt{145+144}=17\\)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/3/aa334ecc3797e7e1604893ea3ba52bb35b9d1834.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\quad\\bullet$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"10\" /> \\(144\\cos\\alpha = 24\\) yields third side lengths of \\(\\sqrt{145-24}=11\\) and \\(\\sqrt{145+24}=13\\)\n</p><p>However, 1 and 17 do not work because they violate the triangle inequality, forming degenerate triangles (\\(8+1=9\\) and \\(8+9=17\\)). Thus, 13 and 11 are the only possible two valid side lengths. We arrive at an answer of \\(11+13=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 24}\\).\n</p><p>(*) This preserves the base of 9 and the height of \\(8\\sin\\alpha\\), ensuring the area remains identical! Man, what can I say? Mamba out. When I see you again.\n</p><p>~Kobe Tribute\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_7_.28.3C30_sec.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7_(&lt;30_sec)\">Solution 7 (&lt;30 sec)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7 (&lt;30 sec)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let us call our missing sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />. By median length formula, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/0/92028ec350456dd5ba4b8f8d1fa7b41305a8181a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2(8^2) = x^2 + y^2 - 2(9^2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"185\" height=\"21\" />, which gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/7/1e70e72d4b67686b80a22735f7680f50c7d2c4ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 + y^2 = 290$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"110\" height=\"20\" />. Since these are both integers, we plug in reasonable numbers, where we see <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/d/1fd4e794caa5b4b5f6026fd0a044f4d30ec28f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x, y) = (11, 13)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"127\" height=\"20\" />. Thus, our sum is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/d/31dbf1d09d104d47a51d759fbb6c756e3991baac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11 + 13 = \\boxed{24}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"114\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=w5t9UArrJj0\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=w5t9UArrJj0</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_by_OmegaLearn.org\">Video Solution 2 by OmegaLearn.org</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 by OmegaLearn.org\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/ziQ53FFpE_M\">https://youtu.be/ziQ53FFpE_M</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3_by_MadMath\">Video Solution 3 by MadMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 3 by MadMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/cGVji7uHKgs\">https://youtu.be/cGVji7uHKgs</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See A